+## Status note, 2026-10-06: the re-test is the run after the second pass (the owner's answer)
+
+Added on 2026-10-08 by the Director (Claude Code agent), plan revision 15, PR #67. The text above is the record
+as of 2026-10-03 and is unchanged. Its title, "passed on hosted Linux on the repository owner's approval", is
+current again as written.
+
+### What happened since the record
+
+- The first strict `net_bench --gate` runs on WP-0.4's `win-gpu` runner (the owner's PC) measured the
+  encrypted stack below 100k packets per core: 82,842 (run 37231118477, 2026-10-04), then 82,134 (37263321224),
+  89,080 (37328633862) and 85,054 (37340158971, the scheduled run), the last three on 2026-10-05. WP-0.13r
+  (#62) was the second pass that followed.
+- The `win-gpu` run after it, 37391930751 (2026-10-06, on 2bc2d85), **passed strictly: 118,253 packets per core**
+  (8.46 µs of CPU per packet; raw datagrams 215,488 per core; NS-0.7 passed).
+- "The re-test owed" (above) and 09 §5.6 ("If the re-test fails, the approval lapses") leave open which run is
+  the re-test. The first two revisions of PR #67 read it as every strict run on that hardware from the first,
+  which would have lapsed the approval on 2026-10-04, and put that reading to the owner.
+
+### The owner's answer, verbatim
+
+The Director asked on 2026-10-06 (14:27 UTC). The question, as the question tool returned it:
+
+> NS-0.2's 2026-09-30 approval for hosted Linux lapsed on 2026-10-04. The first fixed-hardware re-test on
+> win-gpu measured 82,842 packets per core, under the 100k bar. After the #62 rework, win-gpu passed strictly
+> at 118,253. Hosted Linux swings between about 88k and 110–132k depending on the host it lands on. What
+> should the record say?
+
+The owner selected **"(d) Re-test = post-#62 run"**. The option's description, as the owner saw it:
+
+> You meant 'the re-test' to be the run after the second pass, not the first win-gpu run. The approval never
+> lapsed, the strict pass at 118,253 satisfies it, and the rows are reworded to match.
+
+The other options offered were "(a) Re-confirm as is", "(b) Re-confirm with a floor" and "(c) Leave it lapsed".
+
+### What the record says now
+
+- The approval of 2026-09-30 is in force, as the option the owner chose says: it never lapsed.
+- The re-test it owed on fixed hardware, in the owner's answer, is the `win-gpu` run after the second pass,
+  37391930751, and its strict pass at 118,253 satisfies it. The four runs before it are the measurements that
+  led to the second pass.
+
+### What stays true
+
+- **The Linux half.** "The re-test owed" also names a Linux lab host (owners WP-0.4 and WP-0.13). No Linux lab
+  host exists, and the owner's answer does not say that part is dropped, so the record's follow-up stays as
+  worded, an item for the owner, and stays listed in 09 §8.1 and in `scorecard.jsonc`.
+- **Hosted Windows** (`windows-vs2026`) was never covered by this approval and is not covered now. Its
+  nightly step stays strict, and it passed NS-0.2's stack clause on its own in every run since WP-0.13r: 125,630
+  (nightly 37449776267, attempt 1, 2026-10-06), 134,410 (the owner's re-run of it, attempt 2), 163,699
+  (37606845041, 2026-10-07) and 130,645 (37765109895, 2026-10-08) packets per core. Those figures are
+  in 09 §8.1's addendum of 2026-10-08, with their job logs.
+- **Enforcement.** The nightly's `linux-gcc` step still passes `--advisory ns02-stack`, and `scorecard.jsonc`
+  still has NS-0.2 as `approved`, citing this record. A Director PR changes neither, and the owner's answer
+  does not ask for either to change.
+- **Phase exits.** 09 §5.6's "Phase exits" bullet is unchanged. This note does not decide how the Linux half
+  counts at the Phase 0 exit audit.
+- **Later runs.** After the answer, the scheduled `win-gpu` run of 2026-10-07 (37639107710) measured the stack
+  at 73,622 packets per core on a machine whose own cross-check calls it "not idle" (background 10.40 cores
+  before the stack run, of 12), and its Build step failed with MSVC "compiler is out of heap space" and
+  MSBuild "The paging file is too small" errors; the scheduled runs of 2026-10-06 and 2026-10-08 and the
+  dispatch of 2026-10-06 passed strictly (115,685, 117,645 and 119,214). Neither 09 §5.6 nor this record words
+  what a strict run below 100k after the re-test means for an approval in force, and the owner's answer
+  concerns the re-test. The Director records the run as data, does not read it as a new re-test, and lists the
+  question in 09 §8.2 for the owner.
